      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for the breakdown. It's interesting that you pointed out Marcus and Andre feel like they belong in that \"gritty\" or \"street-level\" category while Julian and Claire go straight to prestige/high-society. \n\nI guess I'm wondering if that's just because of the settings I described, or if there's a certain \"vibe\" or demographic expectation tied to those tropes? Like, do you think audiences would find it jarring if I moved Marcus or Andre into that penthouse setting, or does it just feel inherently \"off\" to write them that way based on how these characters are usually portrayed in media? I'm trying to figure out if I'm fighting against some kind of natural storytelling standard for these types of characters.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You've tapped into a significant aspect of storytelling: the expectations and associations audiences develop with certain character types and narrative scenarios. These expectations often stem from repetitive portrayals in media that have built recognizable archetypes over time. For instance, Marcus, with his neighborhood connections, might initially feel out of place in a penthouse setting because this contrasts sharply with the common image of someone \"connected\" as a leader or influential figure at the grassroots level of society (akin to Tony Montana in \"Scarface\"). Audiences typically expect these neighborhood figures to have tangible, direct impact on the world they govern and often in less affluent, more volatile surroundings.\n\nLikewise, Andre's background suggests a life lived beyond sterile walls, which clashes with the detached haven a penthouse represents. His association with a gritty lifestyle isn't just in the physical sights but in the lived experiences that are markedly different from those behind closed doors in posh neighborhoods. Employing these settings could work as a subversion of expectations—viewers would note the contrast if your characters' lifestyles were as comfortable and detached as those in high society, it would feel dissonant and could be seen as a lack of continuity with their established lives.\n\nIt’s not just about the physical settings; it’s about the",
      "source": "target",
